# GitHub Actions Best Practices

## Security: Pin Actions to Commit SHA

```yaml
# BAD — mutable tag, vulnerable to tag hijacking
- uses: actions/checkout@v4

# GOOD — immutable SHA with version comment
- uses: actions/checkout@11bd71901bbe5b1630ceea73d27597364c9af683 # v4.2.2
```

Pin ALL third-party actions to a full commit SHA. This prevents supply chain attacks via mutable tags.

## Permissions: Principle of Least Privilege

```yaml
permissions: {} # deny all at workflow level

jobs:
  build:
    permissions:
      contents: read # only what this job needs
```

- Set `permissions: {}` at workflow level to deny all by default
- Grant minimum needed permissions at the job level
- Never use `write-all`

## Concurrency: Cancel Stale Runs

```yaml
concurrency:
  group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true
```

## Install uv

```yaml
- name: Install uv
  uses: astral-sh/setup-uv@<sha> # v7
  with:
    version: ${{ env.UV_VERSION }}
    enable-cache: true # automatically keyed on uv.lock
```

## Secrets

- Never `echo ${{ secrets.MY_SECRET }}`
- Pass secrets via `env:` on a step, not inline in `run:` commands:
  ```yaml
  - name: Deploy
    env:
      API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}
    run: ./deploy.sh
  ```

## Job Design

- One concern per job: lint, test, build, deploy as separate jobs
- Use `needs:` to express explicit dependencies: `needs: [lint, test]`
- Set `timeout-minutes:` on every job to prevent runaway billing
